{
  "abstract": "A general theorem and its six instances. The theorem (congr_of): let k = 12 + 4a + 6b; if the space M_{k−12} of level-one modular forms has rank one and 2k/B_k = D/ℓ with ℓ and D coprime integers, then the q-expansion coefficients of Δ·E₄^a·E₆^b are integers congruent to σ_{k−1}(n) modulo ℓ, for every n. The hypothesis holds exactly for k = 12, 16, 18, 20, 22, 26, where the cusp space is spanned by this one form, and the instances are the congruences modulo the numerator of B_k/2k: 691, 3617, 43867, 174611 = 283·617, 77683 = 131·593 and 657931. The case k = 12 is Ramanujan's τ(n) ≡ σ₁₁(n) (mod 691); the other five are the remaining congruences of this kind at level one. The statements are for every n and use only Mathlib definitions; the Bernoulli numbers B_12 to B_26 are computed in Lean, not assumed. Integrality comes from the product formula for Δ, expanded by Mathlib's pentagonal number theorem, and the integer coefficients 240σ₃ and −504σ₅ of E₄ and E₆. The congruence comes from one exact identity in M_k: E₄^(a+3)·E₆^b − E_k vanishes at the cusp, so dividing by Δ (Mathlib's discriminantEquiv) gives a form in M_{k−12}, which is one-dimensional and spanned by E₄^a·E₆^b; hence E₄^(a+3)·E₆^b − E_k = c·Δ·E₄^a·E₆^b, with c read off from the q¹-coefficient. Since E_k = 1 − (2k/B_k)∑σ_{k−1}(n)qⁿ, multiplying by the numerator of B_k/2k and reducing shows that the Eisenstein series is congruent to the cusp form. The argument is proved once for general (a, b) and applied to the six cases.",
